  "final_text": "**No. Fix CRAFT first and hold off on extra Q3 marketing.** Both of the VP's arguments come from how the numbers are counted. When you count them per week or in units affected, neither holds up.\n\n## The two claims, checked against the raw data\n\n**1. \"Units up 22.4% on August\"**\n- **What's counted:** CRAFT units shipped, total for the month. Source: `demand_actuals.csv` `units_actual`, report §2.\n- **Period:** September has 5 weekly buckets (09-02 to 09-30). August has 4 (08-05 to 08-26).\n- **Limit:** a month with more weeks will look bigger. Report §6 says this itself.\n\n| CRAFT | Aug (4 wks) | Sep (5 wks) | Change |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Monthly total | 7,488 | 9,162 | **+22.4%** (reported) |\n| Per week | 1,872 | 1,832 | **−2.1%** |\n\nPer week, CRAFT demand **fell slightly**. Most lines grew a little per week (AURA +0.8%, ECHO +2.8%, BREW +2.4%), so CRAFT is one of the weaker lines, not a breakout.\n\n**2. \"Defects fell from 3.21 to 2.51 per 1,000 units, and no other line improved\"**\n- **What's counted:** the number of defect reports (NCRs). It isn't the number of defective units. Source: `ncr_records.csv`, report §3.\n- **Divided by:** units shipped. That denominator grows by 25% just from the extra week.\n- **Limit:** one report can cover 10 units or 400. This is also production quality, not customer returns.\n\n| CRAFT | Aug | Sep | Direction |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| Defect reports per 1,000 units (reported) | 3.21 | 2.51 | better |\n| Defect reports (total) | 24 | 23 | flat |\n| **Units affected** (`qty_affected`) | 2,324 | 2,832 | worse |\n| Units affected as a share of units shipped | **31.0%** | **30.9%** | **flat** |\n| Units scrapped | 924 | 1,539 | worse |\n| Units with weld cracks | 228 | 754 | worse |\n\nThe count of defect reports per week did fall (6.0 to 4.6). But the amount of product affected didn't. About **31% of CRAFT's volume** shows up in defect reports, which is roughly 3× NEST and 5× AURA. Scrap went up. \"No other line improved\" is also wrong: by units affected, NEST improved from 11.8% to 10.5%.\n\n**Check against the raw data:** the CRAFT September figure of 9,162 units comes from 95 rows in `demand_actuals.csv` (CRAFT products, weeks starting in September). The 23 defect reports are 23 rows in `ncr_records.csv` (CRAFT, dated in September). Revenue (€4,124,238) and the 2.51 rate also match exactly. The report's arithmetic is right; the conclusions drawn from it aren't.\n\n## Recommendation\n- **Decision:** put CRAFT rework first. Don't move more Q3 marketing to CRAFT yet.\n- **Why:** on a units-affected basis, about 31% of CRAFT volume appears in defect reports, flat from August to September. Scrap rose from 924 to 1,539 units and weld cracks from 228 to 754. More demand means more of this production. Per-week demand is also slightly down (−2.1%), so there's no clear momentum to push behind.\n- **Risks:**\n  - If you wait, you could lose some premium sales during rework. CRAFT is about €0.8M of list revenue per week.\n  - If you spend now, you pay to sell more of a line that scraps about 17% of its volume. Margin is also unknown because the data has no cost per product (§6). A €450 average price doesn't mean high profit.\n- **The VP's reading:** counting defect reports and comparing monthly totals, quality improved (3.21 to 2.51) and units grew 22.4%. Both are real numbers, but they come from the counting method, not from better quality or faster demand.\n- **What would change the decision:** these are my suggested thresholds, so please confirm or set your own.\n  - Units affected stay under about 10% of CRAFT units shipped (NEST's level) for 4 weeks in a row.\n  - Weld-crack units fall back below August's 228.\n  - Per-week CRAFT demand is flat or up.\n\n  I set these in units rather than defect-report counts on purpose. Merging or splitting reports would change the report count without changing anything on the line.\n\n**What I couldn't check:** profit margin, customer returns by line, and how marketing spend splits by line. None of these are in the data (report §4 and §6).",
